# Sample .env for the GitOps repo compose.  Commit this alongside
# docker-compose.yaml in your GitOps repo — Accelero reads it and
# substitutes ${VAR} references in the compose file before parsing.
#
# ⚠️  THIS FILE IS NOT FOR SECRETS  ⚠️
#
# It is committed to git and read at parse time.  Put ONLY declarative
# config here: image tags, registry names, public ports, feature flags.
# Anything whose leak would be a security incident (DB passwords, API
# keys, access tokens) MUST go through a different path:
#
#   - Git / registry credentials: set via the Accelero API on the stack
#     (repo_token, docker_password — stored in Accelero's SQLite, not in
#     the GitOps repo).
#   - Application secrets (DB passwords, API keys): put them in a
#     separate file referenced by `env_file:` in your compose service,
#     populated by the host at deploy time; that file must be gitignored.
#   - External secret stores (Vault, AWS/GCP Secrets Manager): planned
#     for Phase 4 — see ROADMAP.md.
#
# Do NOT confuse this with `.env.example` at the repo root — that one
# is the template for configuring the Accelero daemon itself (API_KEY,
# server port, etc.).  This file belongs in *your* GitOps repo next to
# your compose.
#
# Supported syntax (from docs/usage-overview.md):
#   KEY=value
#   QUOTED="with \n escapes"
#   LITERAL='literal, no escapes'
#   export EXPORTED=works_too
#   # comments start with #

# --- Used by samples/docker-compose.yaml ---

# Image override; if unset, compose falls back to its :- default.
# NGINX_TAG=nginx:1.27.1-alpine

# Caddy tag — required (compose uses :? to fail fast if missing).
CADDY_TAG=2-alpine

# Number of web replicas. Three is enough to prove round-robin and
# rolling updates across an image bump without being wasteful.
WEB_REPLICAS=3

# Optional port/target overrides with defaults in the compose.
# WEB_PORT=80
# PUBLIC_PORT=80
